  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nThe next incoming trough coming down from Canada will bring an \nincrease in winds aloft today. These stronger winds aloft will \nmix down to the surface from day time vertical mixing. The \ncombination of the warm, breezy, and dry conditons will bring \nelevated to critical fire weather conditions to the region. \nTemperatures are also expected to peak today, with numerous lower \nelevation locations reaching into the 80s. \n\nSaturday, an elongated trough coming in from Canada will bring a \nmulti-day weather system through Montana. A cold front passing \nfrom north to south Saturday morning bring the first rush of \ncooler air to the region along with mountain snow and lower \nelevation rain/snow. Lee troughing with northerly cold air \nadvection continues behind the front, and transitions rain to snow\nat lower elevations by Saturday evening. Meanwhile the front \nprogression slows down heading into Southwest MT, which delays the\nswitch over the snow later than North-Central MT. Strong \nconvergence and frontogenesis also occurs along this front as it\nhead into North-Central/Central MT, which can develop moderate to\nheavy snow banding. Northerly flow in North-Central MT will allow\nfor enhanced snowfall for northerly upslope areas of the Little \nBelts/Central Continental Divide. Temperatures don't look to warm\nup much heading into Sunday. Increasing moisture from the \nremnant southwest tropical storm system will continue to feed \nmoisture into this storm system Sunday into Monday before exiting \nlater in the day Monday.\n\nBehind this system, another quick moving trough looks to swing \nthrough Wednesday into Thursday, which will keep the cooler \ntemperatures and chances of precipitation. However, details with \nthis will be heavily dependent on what happens this \nweekend/Monday. \n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nEven though we are closer in time to this next weather system, there \nremains some uncertainty in the progression of this system. This \nis due to the remnant of the tropical system Rachel lifting north\nfrom the Southwest U.S. and into the Rockies. The first \nuncertainty is the cold front timing Saturday. Some recent trends\nslow down the front progression later into the afternoon Saturday\nacross North-Central MT which will bring a slower transition to \nrain to snow. Some solutions have this earlier cold front timing \nSaturday morning, which bring a quicker transition to rain to \nsnow as temperatures will struggle to heat up with diurnal \ndaytime heating. Another factor is if any moderate to heavy snow \nbanding can dynamically cool the atmosphere quicker, leading to a\nquicker rain to snow transition.\n\nThe second uncertainty is where the better convergence and \nfrontogenesis enhanced snow banding sets up. Models were trending \ntowards Central/North-Central MT, but some recent runs have that \ncold front and surface low stalling by the MT and ID border. As a \nresult, there also could be moderate to heavy snowfall in \nSouthwest MT Sunday into Monday. For Central/North-Central MT,\nany moderate to heavier snow banding can crash surface/road\ntemperatures, leading to quicker accumulations. Probabilities for\n6\" of snow along the Montana Highway 200 corridor and northerly\nupslope areas of Cascade and Judith Basin Counties run 50%\nchances. However, the concerns of these snow bands developing gave\nthe nudges to expand the Winter Storm Watches into those areas \nand the Meagher County Valley. That front stalling out near the \nMT/ID border combined with the warm, moist tropical airmass \noverriding the boundary may also bring concerns for a wintry mix \nalong the state border.\n\nThe main impacts from this weather system will be wet snow \nweighing down power lines and trees, leading to power outages and \ndamaged trees. Temperatures dropping into the 20s Sunday morning \nwill allow for wet pavements to freeze and become slippery. Colder\ntemperatures will also impact any outdoor recreation in the \nmountains/back country and travel over mountain passes will be \nimpacted by heavy, wet snow. -Wilson",
